'''''Paraserianthes lophantha''''' (syn. ''Albizia lophantha''), the '''[[Cape Leeuwin]] wattle''', '''Bicol wattle''', '''Cape wattle''', '''Crested wattle''', '''Brush wattle'''<ref>{{Cite web |last=Council |first=Northland Regional |title=Pest control hub - Northland Regional Council |url=https://www.nrc.govt.nz/environment/weed-and-pest-control/pest-control-hub/?pwsystem=true&pwid=98 |access-date=2024-08-16 |website=www.nrc.govt.nz |language=en-nz}}</ref> or '''plume albizia''', is a fast-growing tree with creamy-yellow, bottlebrush like flowers.<ref name="6subfamilies">{{cite journal | author = The Legume Phylogeny Working Group (LPWG). | year = 2017 | title = A new subfamily classification of the Leguminosae based on a taxonomically comprehensive phylogeny | journal = [[Taxon (journal)|Taxon]] | volume = 66 | issue = 1 | pages = 44–77 | doi = 10.12705/661.3| doi-access = free | hdl = 10568/90658 | hdl-access = free }}</ref> It is the sole species in genus '''''Paraserianthes'''''.<ref>[https://powo.science.kew.org/taxon/urn:lsid:ipni.org:names:893319-1 ''Paraserianthes'' I.C.Nielsen]. ''[[Plants of the World Online]]''. Retrieved 13 September 2023.</ref>

It is a small tree (uppermost height approximately {{Convert|5|m|disp=or}}) that occurs naturally along the [[Southwest Australia|southwest]] coast of [[Western Australia]], from [[Fremantle, Western Australia|Fremantle]] to [[King George Sound]].<ref>{{cite web| url=https://www.anbg.gov.au/gnp/gnp9/paraserianthes-lophantha.html | title=''Paraserianthes lophantha'' (synonym ''Albizia lophantha'') | date=2003-12-16 | accessdate=2008-09-28}}</ref> It is also native to [[Sumatra]], [[Java]], and the [[Lesser Sunda Islands]].<ref name = powo/> It was first spread beyond southwest Australia by Baron [[Ferdinand von Mueller]], who gave packets of ''P.&nbsp;lophantha'' seeds to early explorers under the assumption that if they planted the seeds at their campsites, the trees would indicate the routes they travelled.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.weeds.org.au/cgi-bin/weedident.cgi?tpl=plant.tpl&ibra=all&card=E10# |title= Weeds Australia - Weed Identification - Cape Leeuwin wattle|website=www.weeds.org.au |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080804001332/http://www.weeds.org.au/cgi-bin/weedident.cgi?tpl=plant.tpl&ibra=all&card=E10 |archive-date=2008-08-04}}</ref>

It is considered a weed in the parts of Australia where it is not [[indigenous (ecology)|indigenous]],<ref>{{cite web|title=Weed of the month archive|url=http://www.angair.org.au/index.php?option=com_content&view=article&id=35&Itemid=29|publisher=Angair Inc.|accessdate=2013-08-27}}</ref> as well as in [[New Zealand]], [[South Africa]], the [[Canary Islands]], the [[Philippines]] and [[Chile]].<ref>{{Cite web|title=Paraserianthes lophantha (Willd.) I.C.Nielsen|url=https://www.gbif.org/species/2943719|access-date=2020-10-31|website=www.gbif.org|language=en}}</ref>

==Taxonomy==
It was first described in 1806 as ''Acacia lophantha'' by [[Willdenow]], but was transferred to the genus ''Paraserianthes'' by Nielsen, Guinet and Baretta-Kuipers in 1983.<ref name=apni>{{APNI2|id=|name=Paraserianthes lophantha}}</ref><ref name=nielson>{{cite Q|Q101031463|pages= 326|url=https://www.biodiversitylibrary.org/page/13958676}}</ref>

The genus ''Paraserianthes'' originally comprised four species, divided into two sections based on morphological traits by Nielsen.<ref name="NielsenA">Nielsen, I., Guinet, P., Baretta-Kuipers, T., 1983. Studies in the Malesian, Australian and Pacific Ingeae (Leguminosae - Mimosoideae): the genera Archidendropsis, Wallaceodendron, Paraserianthes, Parachidendron and Serianthes (part 1). Bull. Mus. natl. Hist. nat. Paris, 4, ser. 5, sect. B, Adansonia 3, 303-329.</ref><ref name="NielsenB">Nielsen, I., Guinet, P., Baretta-Kuipers, T., 1983. Studies in the Malesian, Australian and Pacific Ingeae (Leguminosae - Mimosoideae): the genera Archidendropsis, Wallaceodendron, Paraserianthes, Parachidendron and Serianthes (part 2). Bull. Mus. natl. Hist. nat. Paris, 4, ser. 5, sect. B, Adansonia 4, 335-360.</ref> Section ''Paraserianthes'' included ''P.&nbsp;lophantha'' with two recognized [[subspecies]], and section ''Falcataria'' included three species (''P.&nbsp;falcataria, P.&nbsp;pullenii,'' and ''P.&nbsp;toona'').<ref name="NielsenA" /><ref name="NielsenB" />

Based on morphology, ''P. falcataria'' <small>(L.) I.C.Nielsen</small> was moved to the genus ''[[Falcataria]]'' by Barneby and Grimes,<ref name="Barneby">Barneby, R.C., Grimes, J.W., 1996. Silk tree, Guanacaste, Monkey's earring: a generic system for the synandrous Mimoseae of the Americas. Part I. Abarema, Albizia and allies. Mem. New York Botanical Garden 74: 1-292</ref> and renamed ''[[Falcataria moluccana]]'' <small>(Miq.) Barneby & J.W.Grimes</small>.<ref name="Barneby"/> Brown ''et al.'' used biogeographical, morphological and molecular studies to completely separate of these sections into two genera as ''Paraserianthes sensu'' Nielsen was [[paraphyletic]].<ref name="Brown" /> Now ''P.&nbsp;pullenii'' <small>(Verdc.) I.C. Nielsen</small><ref name="NielsenA" /><ref name="NielsenB" /> = ''[[Falcataria pullenii]]'' <small>(Verdc.) G.K. Brown, D.J. Murphy & P.Y. Ladiges</small>;<ref name="Brown" /> and ''P.&nbsp;toona'' <small>(F.M. Bailey) I.C. Nielsen</small><ref name="NielsenA" /><ref name="NielsenB" /> = ''[[Falcataria toona]]'' <small>(Bailey), G.K. Brown, D.J. Murphy & P.Y. Ladiges</small>.<ref name="Brown" /> ''Paraserianthes'' section remained in the genus with only ''P.&nbsp;lophantha''.<ref name="Brown" />

=== Subspecies ===
''Paraserianthes lophantha'' includes two subspecies:<ref name="Brown">Brown, G.K., Murphy, D.J., Ladiges, P.Y., 2011. Relationships of the Australo-Malesian genus Paraserianthes (Mimosoideae: Leguminosae) identifies the sister group of Acacia sensu stricto and two biogeographical tracks. Cladistics 27: 380-390.</ref>
*''P. lophantha'' subsp. ''lophantha'' in southwestern Australia
*''P. lophantha'' subsp. ''montana'' <small>(Jungh.) I.C. Nielsen</small> in high elevation sites in Western Indonesia (Sumatara, Java and Western Nusa Tenggara Province).

